"""In-depth material for the Computer Organisation lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("coa-instruction-addressing",
       proofs=[
           ("Expanding opcodes: counting instructions level by level",
            """With a 16-bit instruction and 4-bit fields, a three-address instruction uses a 4-bit opcode and three 4-bit addresses, so the opcode space has 2⁴ = 16 codes. If x₃ of them are used by three-address instructions, 16 − x₃ remain unused. Each unused 4-bit code can be extended using the first address field: the 4-bit prefix together with the next 4 bits give 16 new 8-bit codes, so the two-address level offers (16 − x₃)·16 codes. If x₂ of those are used, (16 − x₃)·16 − x₂ remain and are extended in the same way: in general the codes available at the next level are (previous available − used)·2⁴. For 14 three-address and 31 two-address instructions: (16 − 14)·16 = 32, then (32 − 31)·16 = 16 one-address codes. If 15 one-address instructions are used, (16 − 15)·16 = 16 zero-address instructions remain. The recurrence aₖ₊₁ = (aₖ − xₖ)·2^f is the whole technique."""),
           ("Instruction width and the cost of an addressing mode",
            """The instruction length is the opcode bits plus the sum of the operand field widths. For 120 opcodes the opcode needs ⌈log₂ 120⌉ = 7 bits; a register field for 64 registers needs 6 bits; a direct address into 16 MB of byte-addressable memory needs 24 bits. An instruction with one register and one memory operand is 7 + 6 + 24 = 37 bits. Number of memory references to fetch an operand: immediate 0, register 0, direct 1, register-indirect 1, indirect 2, indexed 1 (the addition happens in the ALU, not in memory). Relative addressing adds a signed offset to the PC; a k-bit signed offset reaches −2ᵏ⁻¹ … 2ᵏ⁻¹ − 1 instructions (or bytes) around the PC."""),
           ("Zero-, one-, two- and three-address code for X = (A + B) × (C + D)",
            """Three-address: ADD R1, A, B; ADD R2, C, D; MUL X, R1, R2 (3 instructions). Two-address (destination also a source): MOV R1, A; ADD R1, B; MOV R2, C; ADD R2, D; MUL R1, R2; MOV X, R1 (6). One-address (accumulator): LOAD A; ADD B; STORE T; LOAD C; ADD D; MUL T; STORE X (7). Zero-address (stack): PUSH A; PUSH B; ADD; PUSH C; PUSH D; ADD; MUL; POP X (8). Fewer addresses per instruction mean shorter instructions but more of them; stack machines need no register names, which is why expressions in postfix map directly onto them."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Instruction cycle and formats",
            """Fetch: MAR ← PC, MDR ← M[MAR], IR ← MDR, PC ← PC + 1. Decode: the control unit interprets the opcode and addressing mode. Execute: operand fetch, ALU operation, write-back. Interrupts are checked at the end of each instruction. RISC machines use a fixed instruction length, a load–store architecture, many registers and simple addressing modes; CISC machines have variable-length instructions with memory operands and complex modes. Big-endian machines store the most significant byte at the lowest address, little-endian the least significant byte: the 32-bit word 0x12345678 at address 100 occupies bytes 12 34 56 78 (big) or 78 56 34 12 (little)."""),
           ("Addressing modes in use",
            """Immediate (constants), direct (static data), indirect (pointers, doubles the memory accesses), register and register-indirect (fast pointers), **indexed** (EA = base address + index register, array elements), **base-register** (relocation of programs), **PC-relative** (position-independent code and branches), **auto-increment/decrement** (stack operations and walking arrays), displacement (the general form of base plus offset). GATE questions usually give the register contents and a few memory words and ask for the effective address or the operand value; remember to distinguish the two."""),
       ])

deepen("coa-datapath-control-performance",
       proofs=[
           ("Amdahl's law",
            """Let a program spend a fraction F of its time in a part that is sped up by a factor S; the remaining fraction 1 − F is unchanged. If the original time is 1, the new time is (1 − F) + F/S. Therefore Speedup = 1 / ((1 − F) + F/S). As S → ∞ the speedup approaches 1/(1 − F), the limit imposed by the unimproved part. With F = 0.6 and S = 4: 1/(0.4 + 0.15) = 1.82, and even an infinitely fast enhanced part gives at most 2.5. Amdahl's law is why optimising rare operations yields little and why parallel speedups saturate when a serial fraction remains."""),
           ("CPU time equation and weighted CPI",
            """Execution time = (instruction count) × (cycles per instruction) × (clock period). With a mix of instruction classes with frequencies fᵢ (summing to 1) and CPIᵢ, the average CPI is Σ fᵢ CPIᵢ because total cycles = Σ ICᵢ CPIᵢ = IC Σ fᵢ CPIᵢ. For 40% ALU at 1, 30% loads at 2, 10% stores at 2 and 20% branches at 3: CPI = 0.4 + 0.6 + 0.2 + 0.6 = 1.8. A program of 2×10⁹ instructions at 2 GHz runs for 2×10⁹ × 1.8 / (2×10⁹) = 1.8 s. MIPS = clock rate/(CPI × 10⁶), which is misleading when instruction sets differ because the amount of work per instruction differs."""),
           ("Horizontal versus vertical microinstruction width",
            """A control word has one control signal per bit in a horizontal microprogram: with groups of 8, 4 and 5 mutually exclusive signals that is 17 bits and no decoding is required, so many signals can be asserted at the same time. In a vertical microprogram each group of k mutually exclusive signals is encoded in ⌈log₂(k + 1)⌉ bits (the extra code means 'none'), giving ⌈log₂ 9⌉ + ⌈log₂ 5⌉ + ⌈log₂ 6⌉ = 4 + 3 + 3 = 10 bits at the price of decoders and less parallelism. The control store size is (number of microinstructions) × (microinstruction width), and the next-address field adds ⌈log₂(number of microinstructions)⌉ bits unless sequencing is implicit."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Hardwired and microprogrammed control",
            """Hardwired control is a finite-state machine implemented with gates or a PLA: fast and costly to modify, used in RISC processors. Microprogrammed control stores sequences of microinstructions in a ROM control store: slower but flexible and easy to extend, used in CISC processors. A **nanoprogram** adds a second level of control store to reduce its size. Single-cycle designs make the clock as slow as the longest instruction; multi-cycle designs use a shorter clock and a variable number of cycles per instruction; pipelined designs overlap instructions. The bus structure (single-bus, two-bus, three-bus) determines how many micro-operations can happen in one cycle."""),
           ("Performance metrics",
            """Throughput versus latency, clock rate versus CPI, speedup = Time_old/Time_new, MIPS = IC/(time × 10⁶), MFLOPS for floating point. Benchmarks: SPEC, and the geometric mean of ratios is used because it is independent of the reference machine. The number of cycles for an instruction with several components adds, e.g. a load taking 5 cycles in a 5-stage multicycle design. Power ∝ capacitance × voltage² × frequency, so lowering voltage is the most effective way of saving power. Doubling the number of cores helps only the parallelisable fraction."""),
       ])

deepen("coa-pipelining",
       proofs=[
           ("Ideal speedup of a k-stage pipeline",
            """With n instructions and k equal stages of one cycle each, a non-pipelined machine takes n·k cycles. A pipelined machine fills the pipeline in k cycles (the first instruction takes k cycles) and then completes one instruction per cycle, so it needs k + (n − 1) cycles. Speedup = nk/(k + n − 1). As n → ∞ this tends to k; for k = 5 and n = 100: 500/104 = 4.81. Pipelining improves throughput, not the latency of an individual instruction (which is k cycles, or more with the register overhead). Unequal stages are limited by the slowest: the cycle time is the slowest stage plus the register overhead."""),
           ("Stall counting with data hazards",
            """Assume the register file writes in the first half of a cycle and reads in the second half. Without forwarding, a consumer must read the register after the producer's WB: if the producer's ID is in cycle t the producer writes at t + 3, so the consumer's ID cannot be before cycle t + 3. An immediately following dependent instruction would be at t + 1, hence **2 stall cycles**; one instruction between them gives 1 stall; two or more in between give none. With forwarding, an ALU result is available at the end of EX and can feed the next instruction's EX with no stall; a **load** produces its value only after MEM, so a dependent instruction immediately after a load stalls **1** cycle. For LW R1,0(R2); ADD R3,R1,R4; SUB R5,R3,R6; SW R5,0(R7) with no forwarding: three dependencies × 2 stalls + 8 ideal cycles = 14 cycles; with forwarding: 1 stall + 8 = 9 cycles."""),
           ("Effective CPI with branch penalties",
            """If a fraction b of instructions are branches, a fraction t of them cause a penalty of p stall cycles (taken, with predict-not-taken), the CPI is 1 + b·t·p. For b = 0.2, t = 0.6, p = 3: 1 + 0.36 = 1.36. If the branch is resolved in a later stage the penalty p grows; delayed branches or branch prediction reduce it. A one-bit predictor mispredicts twice per loop execution (at the exit and at the next entry); a two-bit saturating counter mispredicts once, so it is better for loops."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Hazard taxonomy",
            """Structural hazards (resource conflicts, e.g. a single memory port used for instruction fetch and data access) are solved by duplicating resources. Data hazards: RAW (true dependency, the main one in in-order pipelines), WAR (anti-dependency) and WAW (output dependency) appear with out-of-order execution and are removed by register renaming. Control hazards: branches; solutions are stalling, predicting, delayed branches and early branch resolution. Forwarding paths: EX/MEM → EX and MEM/WB → EX; the hazard detection unit inserts a bubble for load-use. Precise exceptions require that instructions commit in order."""),
           ("Beyond the five-stage pipeline",
            """Superpipelining uses more, shorter stages; superscalar processors issue several instructions per cycle (CPI < 1); dynamic scheduling (scoreboarding, Tomasulo) executes instructions out of order when operands are ready; VLIW relies on the compiler to pack independent operations; speculation executes past branches and discards the results if the prediction was wrong. The ideal CPI of an n-wide superscalar machine is 1/n. Cache misses and dependencies, not the pipeline depth, usually limit the real CPI."""),
       ])

deepen("coa-cache-memory",
       proofs=[
           ("Splitting the address into tag, index and offset",
            """With block size B (a power of two) the lowest log₂ B address bits select the byte inside the block (the offset). The block number is the address divided by B. In a cache with S sets (S = C/(B·k) for capacity C and associativity k) the block number modulo S selects the set, so the next log₂ S bits form the **index**, and the remaining high bits are the **tag** that distinguishes the many blocks competing for one set. For a 32-bit address, a 16 KB 4-way cache and 32-byte blocks: offset 5 bits, sets = 16384/(32·4) = 128 so the index is 7 bits, tag 32 − 7 − 5 = 20 bits. A fully associative cache has one set (no index bits); a direct-mapped cache has k = 1, so the number of sets equals the number of lines."""),
           ("Average memory access time, single and multi-level",
            """AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty, because every access pays the hit time and the fraction that miss additionally pay the penalty. For two levels the miss penalty of L1 is itself the AMAT of L2: AMAT = t₁ + m₁(t₂ + m₂·t_mem). With t₁ = 1, m₁ = 0.1, t₂ = 10, m₂ = 0.2 (local), t_mem = 100: 1 + 0.1(10 + 20) = 4 ns. Including the memory stalls in the CPI: CPI = base CPI + (memory references per instruction) × miss rate × miss penalty (in cycles). Splitting L1 into instruction and data caches removes structural conflicts in the pipeline."""),
           ("Why a direct-mapped cache can be worse than a set-associative one on the same trace",
            """Blocks that map to the same line in a direct-mapped cache evict each other even when the rest of the cache is idle (**conflict misses**). With a 64-byte cache of 16-byte blocks the blocks 0 and 4 collide in line 0; the trace 0, 16, 32, 48, 64, 0, 16, 80, 0, 64 gives 2 hits and 8 misses. Making the same cache 2-way associative (2 sets of 2 lines) lets blocks 0 and 4 coexist, and the same trace then yields 3 hits and 7 misses under LRU. Fully associative caches have no conflict misses; the remaining misses are compulsory (first touch) or capacity misses (the working set is larger than the cache)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Write policies and replacement",
            """Write-through updates the cache and memory on every write (simple, needs a write buffer), write-back updates only the cache and marks the line dirty, writing it back on eviction (less traffic, needs a dirty bit). On a write miss: write-allocate fetches the block first, no-write-allocate writes directly to memory. Common pairings: write-back + write-allocate and write-through + no-write-allocate. Replacement: LRU, FIFO, random; LRU needs log₂ k! bits per set to record the order for k-way caches. Inclusion property: L2 contains everything in L1 (inclusive) or is exclusive of it."""),
           ("Miss classification and block-size effects",
            """Compulsory (cold) misses happen on the first access to a block; capacity misses happen because the cache is smaller than the working set (they occur even in a fully associative cache); conflict misses happen because of limited associativity. Larger blocks exploit spatial locality and reduce compulsory misses but increase the miss penalty and can increase conflict misses; larger caches reduce capacity misses; higher associativity reduces conflict misses but lengthens the hit time. Main-memory interleaving with m modules delivers m consecutive words in roughly one access time plus (m − 1) transfer cycles."""),
       ])

deepen("coa-io-interrupts-dma",
       proofs=[
           ("Fraction of the CPU consumed by interrupt-driven I/O versus DMA",
            """A device delivers data at rate r bytes per second in words of w bytes, causing r/w interrupts per second. If servicing one interrupt takes s seconds of CPU time, the CPU fraction is (r/w)·s. For r = 100,000 B/s, w = 4 and s = 5 µs: 25,000 × 5 µs = 0.125, i.e. 12.5%. With DMA moving blocks of b bytes the CPU is involved once per block (programming the controller and handling the completion interrupt, total c seconds): the fraction is (r/b)·c. For b = 4000 and c = 100 µs: 25 × 100 µs = 2.5 ms per second = 0.25%. So DMA reduces the CPU load by a factor of about 50 here, and the advantage grows with the block size."""),
           ("Cycle stealing versus burst mode",
            """In burst (block) mode the DMA controller takes the bus for the whole block, so the CPU cannot access memory during that time. In cycle-stealing mode it takes the bus for one word at a time between CPU memory cycles, slowing the CPU a little but never blocking it for long. If each word transfer holds the bus for τ and the device supplies one word every T, the fraction of memory bus time used by the DMA is τ/T and the CPU keeps 1 − τ/T of the memory bandwidth. Transparent DMA uses the bus only in cycles the CPU does not need it, at no cost to the CPU."""),
           ("Interrupt response: priority and latency",
            """On an interrupt the CPU completes the current instruction, saves the program counter and flags, identifies the device (polling, a vector supplied by the device, or a daisy chain in which the device closest to the CPU has the highest priority), jumps to the interrupt service routine and later returns by restoring the saved state. **Interrupt latency** = time to finish the current instruction + time to save the state + time to identify the source. Masking lets the CPU ignore lower-priority interrupts during a critical routine; non-maskable interrupts signal serious events. Traps (system calls, page faults, arithmetic exceptions) are synchronous with the executing instruction; device interrupts are asynchronous."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Programmed I/O, interrupt-driven I/O and memory mapping",
            """With programmed I/O the CPU polls a status register until the device is ready (busy waiting) and moves every word itself. Interrupt-driven I/O lets the CPU run other work until the device interrupts, but the CPU still copies each word. DMA lets the controller move the data directly between device and memory. Memory-mapped I/O assigns device registers to memory addresses so ordinary loads and stores are used (reducing the address space available to memory); isolated I/O uses a separate I/O address space with special IN/OUT instructions. Buses: synchronous (common clock) versus asynchronous (handshake), arbitration by daisy chain, polling or independent request."""),
           ("Bandwidth calculations",
            """Bus bandwidth = width (bytes) × clock rate × transfers per clock: a 64-bit bus at 100 MHz with double data rate gives 8 × 200 M = 1.6 GB/s. A disk transfer time = block size / transfer rate; seek and rotational delays dominate for small blocks. When a device interrupts for every word, the maximum sustainable data rate is the word size divided by the interrupt-service time. For DMA, include the bus-arbitration time per block."""),
       ])
